course: dictionary
lesson: 7355
title: Places where children play
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A playground is a place outside where children play, with swings, slides and climbing frames. When you meet the word, look at the words around it for clues. «A girl in Vermont plays on the icy playground» tells you it is outside, because she is on icy ground.
explain: A school playground is the yard where pupils play at break. Break time is the short rest from lessons, and the playground is where you run about and play with friends.
explain: Writers also use playground to describe things that happen among children. «It is like a playground rumour that "school's closed tomorrow".» A playground rumour is a story children pass around that may not be true. Ask who said it, how they know, and whether a teacher says the same.
fact: A playground is a place outside where children play.
fact: Playgrounds often have swings, slides and climbing frames.
fact: A school playground is the yard where pupils play at break.
fact: In the USA, break time at school is called recess. In the UK, it is called break time.
fact: A playground rumour is a story passed round among children that may not be true.
exercise: «A girl in Vermont plays on the icy playground.» Where is she playing? | Under her bed | In a swimming pool | In a shop | *Outside, in a playground
exercise: «It is like a playground rumour that "school's closed tomorrow".» What is a playground rumour? | A game played with a ball | *A story passed around by children that may not be true | A rule the teacher writes on the board | A map of the school yard
exercise: What does the dictionary say a school playground is? | A hall with rows of desks | *The yard where pupils play at break | A room where pupils write | A bus that takes pupils home
test:
Which of these would you find in a playground? | A bath | *A slide | A fridge | A wardrobe
What do Americans call break time at school? | Homework | Assembly | *Recess | Lunchbox
exam:
Which of these belongs in a playground? | Oven | Bookshelf | *Climbing frame | Wardrobe
